{#
  Webinar Last -- theme Witty
  Le fichier est du MJML : GrapesJsController bascule en mode MJML des qu'il
  trouve <mjml> dans le rendu (email.mjml.twig est marque deprecie dans le
  code de Mautic).

  Attention en editant : ce fichier passe par Twig. Une double accolade y
  serait interpretee comme une variable Twig. Ce theme n'en a aucune : les
  emplacements sont ecrits [ENTRE CROCHETS], a remplacer dans le builder --
  meme convention que Webinar Day 0 et Webinar Landing Page. Les tokens
  Mautic ({contactfield=...}, {webview_url}) n'ont qu'une accolade et
  traversent Twig sans dommage.
#}
<mjml>
  <!--
  ==========================================================================
  WEBINAR TEMPLATE -- REMINDER / LAST CALL
  Reminder / last-call email before a webinar (adaptable to any niche)
  ==========================================================================

  SIX RULES BEFORE YOU WRITE
  1. ONE goal only: the click to the registration page. No links to your
     blog, socials, or homepage. Every extra link splits your click rate.
  2. ONE destination: all 3 buttons point to the SAME URL. The wording
     changes, the promise doesn't.
  3. Write to ONE person, not to a list. "You", never "you all". The reader
     should feel this was written for them.
  4. Sell the REGISTRATION, not the webinar. This email's job is the click;
     the landing page sells the content.
  5. Benefit over feature. Not "we'll cover X", but "you'll leave with X,
     able to do Y".
  6. Text/image ratio: keep at least 60% real text. An image-heavy email
     lands in spam and shows up blank when images are blocked (~40% of
     opens).

  HOW TO USE THIS FILE
  - Every block has a [PLACEHOLDER] plus a WHY / HOW comment above it.
  - Never reorder the sections: they follow the reading path
    (attention -> problem -> promise -> proof -> action).
  - Before sending, Ctrl+F for "[" -- if anything is left, you're not done.
  ==========================================================================
  -->

  <mj-head>

    <!--
    Attributs par defaut MJML (mj-divider/mj-button) : safe a definir ici
    plutot que balise par balise, aucune des deux n'a de css-class qui
    pourrait entrer en conflit avec cette valeur par defaut.
    - mj-divider 1px (au lieu des 4px par defaut MJML — "le filet trop gros").
    - mj-button 16px (au lieu des 13px par defaut MJML — lisibilite CTA).
    NE PAS faire pareil pour mj-text : ses classes (.h1/.h2/.h3...) definissent
    chacune leur propre line-height dans le <mj-style> ci-dessous, et un
    defaut mj-attributes se compile en style inline qui gagnerait contre
    elles. Le line-height des paragraphes simples est donc pose directement
    sur chaque <mj-text> concerne, plus bas dans le corps.
    -->
    <mj-attributes>
      <mj-divider border-width="1px" border-color="#e5e5e5" />
      <mj-button font-size="16px" />
    </mj-attributes>

    <mj-style>
      @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Poppins:wght@300;400;500;600;700;800&amp;family=Inter:wght@300;400;500;600;700&amp;display=swap');
      .h1 { font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif; font-size: 30px; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.25; letter-spacing: -0.8px; color: #000000; }
      .h2 { font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif; font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.35; letter-spacing: -0.4px; color: #000000; }
      .h3 { font-family: 'Poppins', sans-serif; font-size: 18px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.4; color: #000000; }
      .meta { font-size: 14px; font-weight: 500; color: #666666; }
      .micro { font-size: 12px; font-weight: 400; color: #999999; }
      .strong { font-weight: 600; }
      a { color: #000000; text-decoration: underline; }
      @media only screen and (max-width:480px) {
        .h1 { font-size: 25px !important; letter-spacing: -0.5px !important; }
        .h3 { font-size: 16px !important; }
      }
    </mj-style>
  </mj-head>

  <mj-body width="640px" background-color="#ffffff">

    <!-- ====================================================================
    1. LOGO
    WHY: sender recognition in under half a second. A reader who identifies
    the brand before reading drops their guard. A clean logo on white also
    signals "legitimate business email" to spam filters.
    HOW: 160-200px wide, transparent PNG, hosted on your own domain (never
    Drive or Dropbox -- those links break). Always fill in the alt text.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 28px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-image src="http://mautic.digitalrocket.pt/media/images/logo-digitalrocket.png" width="180px" alt="[Brand name]" fluid-on-mobile="true" />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section>
      <mj-column>
        <mj-divider />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    2. HOOK VISUAL (GIF or image)
    WHY: the brain processes an image ~60,000x faster than a sentence. This
    visual is not decoration: it must announce THE TOPIC or THE FORMAT (the
    speaker's face, a screen capture of the method, a preview of the bonus).
    A light GIF catches the eye, but a visual that says nothing wastes two
    seconds of attention -- and loses the reader.
    HOW: 600px wide (16:9 -> 600x338). GIF under 1 MB, JPG under 200 KB.
    Descriptive alt text is mandatory: if the image is blocked, the alt must
    still sell. Avoid text baked into the image (unreadable on mobile, hurts
    deliverability).
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="28px 24px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-image src="https://via.placeholder.com/600x338/000000/ffffff?text=VISUAL+600x338" width="600px" alt="[Describe the visual: what the reader will see or learn]" fluid-on-mobile="true" />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    3. TITLE BLOCK -- THE PROMISE
    This is the area that decides everything: 80% of readers will only read
    this block. It holds 4 elements, in this exact order. Skip none of them.
    Heading hierarchy on purpose: h1 (headline) -> h3 (subhead), both styled
    as real headings rather than body text, so the block reads as impactful
    at a glance instead of a wall of paragraphs. (Webinar Day 0 additionally
    opens on an h2 hook line above the headline -- this template has no
    equivalent line, so it starts directly on h1.)
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="16px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>

        <!--
        3a. MAIN HEADLINE
        WHY: it must create tension (urgency, curiosity or stakes). The
        brain opens a loop it needs to close, so it keeps reading.
        HOW: 6 to 12 words. Pick ONE angle:
          - Urgency  : "Last call: tomorrow at [time]"
          - Curiosity: "What [audience] still gets wrong in [year]"
          - Stakes   : "Your [asset] will lose [X%] if you do nothing"
          - Outcome  : "[Concrete result] in [timeframe], without [pain]"
        BAN: "Newsletter #12", "Invitation to our webinar", "Don't miss this
        event" -> zero tension, zero clicks.
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="h1" align="center" padding-bottom="16px">
          [HEADLINE -- 6 to 12 words: urgency, curiosity or stakes. Ex. "Last call: tomorrow at 10:30"]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        3b. SUBHEADLINE -- THE "WHAT" IN ONE SENTENCE
        WHY: the headline creates the tension, the subheadline pays it off.
        It answers "what do I concretely get?".
        HOW: one sentence, 12-20 words:
        [action verb] + [measurable result] + [without / in under / even if].
        Ex. "We'll show you how to [result] in [time], even if [objection]".
        Stay concrete: a number always beats an adjective.
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="h3" align="center" padding-bottom="14px">
          [SUBHEADLINE -- 1 sentence: the concrete result + the constraint lifted. Ex. "How to produce X in under 1 hour, even without a team"]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        3c. SOCIAL PROOF
        WHY: conformity bias. "2,600 people already took it" removes
        perceived risk better than any argument.
        HOW: a REAL number. By decreasing power: attendees > customers >
        average rating > a known client name. No number yet? Use experience
        ("12 years doing X") or delete the block. Never invent one.
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="meta" align="center" padding-bottom="18px">
          [SOCIAL PROOF -- one verifiable number. Ex. "Already followed by 2,600+ [audience]"]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        3d. COST OF ENTRY
        WHY: the reader unconsciously asks "how much time will this cost
        me?". Stating 2 minutes removes that friction before it appears.
        HOW: be honest (~200 words per minute). You can also stack it:
        "(2 min read -- 45 min for the webinar)".
        -->
        <mj-text css-class="micro" align="center">
          [COST OF ENTRY -- ex. "(reading time: 2 min)"]
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    4. CTA #1 -- FOR THOSE ALREADY CONVINCED
    WHY: ~15% of readers are ready to click as soon as they've read the
    promise. Forcing them through 400 more words loses them. This top CTA
    captures them at no cost to everyone else.
    HOW: the line above it repeats WHEN (time scarcity). The button label is
    written in the FIRST PERSON ("I'm saving my seat"): the reader reads
    their own commitment, which lifts clicks. Action verb + benefit, never
    "Click here" / "Learn more" (too vague, no promise).
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text align="center" font-family="'Poppins', sans-serif" font-size="15px" font-weight="700" color="#000000" padding-bottom="18px">
          [DATE REMINDER -- ex. "🟢 WEBINAR | Tomorrow at 10:30"]
        </mj-text>
        <mj-button href="[REGISTRATION_URL]">
          [CTA 1 -- 3 to 6 words, first person. Ex. "I'm saving my seat"]
        </mj-button>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-divider />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    5. BODY -- THE P.A.S. STRUCTURE
    Problem -> Agitation -> Solution. The sequence that converts best in
    cold email as well as in reminders: name the pain BEFORE offering the
    cure. A reader who recognises themselves in the problem grants you
    authority on the solution.
    RULE: one idea per paragraph, two lines max. Dense blocks kill mobile
    reading -- white space isn't emptiness, it's pacing.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>

        <!--
        5a. PERSONALISED GREETING
        WHY: the "cocktail party" effect -- a first name grabs attention
        automatically. Measurable: +15 to 25% engagement.
        HOW: ALWAYS use a default value with the pipe, otherwise contacts
        with no first name get "Hi ,". Mautic syntax:
        {contactfield=firstname|there}
        -->
        <mj-text line-height="1.6" padding-bottom="20px">
          Hi <span class="atwho-inserted" data-fr-verified="true">{contactfield=firstname|there}</span>,
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        5b. PROBLEM -- the observation
        WHY: open on the reader's reality, not on yourself. They must think
        "that's exactly my situation" from the first line.
        HOW: one short, affirmative sentence, no hedging. A truth your
        audience feels but has never put into words.
        BAN: "We're delighted to invite you..." (talking about yourself =
        stop).
        -->
        <mj-text font-weight="400" line-height="1.6" padding-bottom="14px">
          [PROBLEM -- 1 observation sentence describing the reader's reality. Ex. "In [year], the rules of [field] changed."]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        5c. AGITATION -- the cost of doing nothing
        WHY: loss aversion is ~2x more motivating than the lure of gain.
        Describing what inaction costs creates movement.
        HOW: 2-3 sentences. Name the wasted effort, the lost time, the gap
        widening with competitors. Stay factual: exaggerated fear triggers
        rejection, not action.
        -->
        <mj-text line-height="1.6" padding-bottom="20px">
          [AGITATION -- 2 to 3 sentences on the cost of inaction: wasted effort, lost time, accumulated delay.]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        5d. TRANSITION -- the solution arrives
        WHY: a very short pivot sentence restarts the reading after the
        negative passage and positions you as the one holding the answer.
        -->
        <mj-text font-weight="400" line-height="1.6" padding-bottom="20px">
          [TRANSITION -- 1 short sentence. Ex. "Tomorrow, we fix that together."]
        </mj-text>

        <!--
        5e. THE APPOINTMENT -- date, time, promise
        WHY: this is the operational information. It must be spottable while
        skimming (bold + emoji anchor), because that's exactly what a
        scanning reader is looking for.
        HOW: day + date + time + timezone for international audiences + the
        promise in bold. Check the date on EVERY reuse of this template: an
        inconsistent date kills all credibility.
        -->
        <mj-text font-weight="600" line-height="1.7">
          📅 [DAY + DATE + TIME] -- [the promise in one line: <span class="strong">"the [method / system] that [result]"</span>]
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    6. CTA #2 -- RIGHT AFTER THE PROMISE
    WHY: this is the intent peak. The reader has just understood what they
    get: don't make them hunt for where to click.
    HOW: same URL, slightly different label (variation avoids the copy-paste
    feel and lets you test which wording clicks best).
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="32px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-button href="[REGISTRATION_URL]">
          [CTA 2 -- variation on CTA 1. Ex. "I'm saving my seat now 🔥"]
        </mj-button>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-divider />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    7. AGENDA -- PROOF IT'S CONCRETE
    WHY: the promise alone stays abstract. 3 concrete points make it
    credible and kill the "another empty webinar" objection. Three isn't
    decorative: two feels thin, five or more dilutes and stops being
    memorable.
    HOW: each bullet is a BENEFIT or a DESTROYED OBJECTION, never a topic.
    Recommended structure:
      Bullet 1 -> why what the reader does today no longer works
      Bullet 2 -> what changed / what they don't know yet
      Bullet 3 -> the method they'll leave with (most desirable one last)
    BAD: "Introduction to the topic"  ->  GOOD: "Why 80% of [X] stall out"
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text font-family="'Poppins', sans-serif" font-size="16px" font-weight="600" padding-bottom="18px">
          What we'll cover:
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text line-height="1.85">
          <ul style="margin:0 0 0 18px;padding:0;list-style:none;">
            <li style="margin-bottom:12px;">✅ [BULLET 1 -- why the reader's current method no longer works. Add a number if you can.]</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom:12px;">✅ [BULLET 2 -- what changed / what they don't know yet.]</li>
            <li>✅ [BULLET 3 -- <span class="strong">the concrete method</span> they'll leave with, plus the measurable result.]</li>
          </ul>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    8. BONUS -- RECIPROCITY + ATTENDANCE
    WHY: double effect. (1) Reciprocity: getting something free creates a
    psychological debt that lifts registrations. (2) "For those who stay to
    the end" lifts the show-up rate -- the real KPI of a webinar, not
    sign-ups.
    HOW: the bonus must be an ASSET usable on its own (template, audit,
    checklist, script, access), not "advice". Name it precisely: "the Notion
    template we use with our 150 clients" beats "a gift". Add proof or
    scarcity on the next line.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="32px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text css-class="h2" padding-bottom="12px">
          🎁 [BONUS -- named precisely. Ex. "The [template/checklist/audit] offered to everyone who stays to the end"]
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text line-height="1.6">
          [PROOF OR SCARCITY -- 1 line. Ex. "Already used with more than 150 clients." or "Not downloadable anywhere else."]
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-divider />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    9. CLOSING + CTA #3 -- FOR SCANNERS
    WHY: some readers scroll straight to the bottom. This last block must
    work ON ITS OWN: nudge + button. It's also the moment for the final
    micro urgency (limited seats, no guaranteed replay, registrations close
    tonight) -- as long as it's TRUE. A fake scarcity, once spotted, costs
    you trust for every campaign that follows.
    HOW: one sentence + button. No new argument here, you're closing.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="36px 24px 0px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text line-height="1.6">
          [CLOSING -- 1 sentence with a TRUE urgency. Ex. "Registrations close tonight -- grab your seat:"]
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section padding="24px 24px 40px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-button href="[REGISTRATION_URL]">
          [CTA 3 -- last variation. Ex. "I'm taking my seat 🔐"]
        </mj-button>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <mj-section>
      <mj-column>
        <mj-divider />
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!-- ====================================================================
    10. FOOTER -- LEGAL AND DELIVERABILITY
    WHY: the unsubscribe link is mandatory (GDPR / CAN-SPAM) and watched by
    Gmail and Outlook. An easy unsubscribe beats a spam complaint: a single
    "spam" hit weighs ~100x more than an unsubscribe on your sending
    reputation.
    The postal address line was removed on purpose (account owner's call).
    Note for future editors: CAN-SPAM (US) and several other jurisdictions
    still expect a physical postal address in every commercial email -- if
    this list ever sends to those regions, put it back rather than assume
    its absence was an oversight.
    HOW: do not touch the {webview_url} and {unsubscribe_url} tokens. Keep
    this block discreet (grey, small) but never hidden.
    ==================================================================== -->
    <mj-section padding="32px 24px 40px">
      <mj-column>
        <mj-text align="center" font-family="'Poppins', sans-serif" font-size="15px" font-weight="600" padding-bottom="14px">
          [BRAND NAME]
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text align="center" css-class="micro" padding-bottom="14px">
          This email was sent to <span class="atwho-inserted" data-fr-verified="true">{contactfield=email}</span>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text align="center" font-size="12px">
          <a href="{webview_url}" style="color:#000000;text-decoration:underline;">View in browser</a> &nbsp;|&nbsp;
          <a href="{unsubscribe_url}" style="color:#000000;text-decoration:underline;">Unsubscribe</a>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>

    <!--
    ==========================================================================
    PRE-SEND CHECKLIST (this block is a comment -- leave it or delete it)
    □ The 3 buttons point to the same URL, with UTM parameters.
    □ Date, day and time checked and consistent across subject, body and LP.
    □ Every [BRACKET] replaced -- run Ctrl+F on "[".
    □ {contactfield=firstname|there} tested with a contact with NO first name.
    □ Test send opened on mobile with images off: the email stays
      understandable and the CTA stays visible.
    □ Alt text filled in on every image.
    □ Subject line under 45 characters, preheader extends it.
    ==========================================================================
    -->

  </mj-body>
</mjml>
